    "summary": "Epic Dark Star marathon capped by soaring Main Ten jam",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a legendary Dark Star/Other One marathon that flows into an inspired Uncle John’s Band jam and the early “Main Ten” improvisation. China Cat > Rider and the Saint Stephen → The Eleven → Caution sequence add extra sparkle. Overall the band is locked in, offering a peak‑late‑’69 performance.",
    "review": "On November 8, 1969 at San Francisco’s Fillmore Auditorium the Dead unleashed one of their most daring late‑‘60s outings. After a solid first set the second half erupts into an extended Dark Star that weaves in and out of The Other One, Uncle John’s Band jam, Saint Stephen, The Eleven, and Caution before launching into the early incarnation of “Playing In The Band” – the Main Ten jam that hints at future directions. The band rides a wave of improvisational intensity, highlighted by a bright China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue and an inspired China Cat > Rider jam later in the set. Pigpen’s organ textures and Tom Constanten’s keyboards add depth to the sprawling jams while Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann drive the rhythmic engine with twin‑drum power. Jerry’s guitar, though tentative at the outset, blossoms into soaring leads that propel the marathon sections, and Phil’s bass provides a steady anchor throughout. This performance captures the Dead in metamorphosis – raw, exploratory, yet remarkably cohesive.",
